**Theorem 14.4 (obstruction required by a genuine nine-low point).**
Retain Assumption 1.1 and the same-root enclosure premises of
Assumption 10.1. Fix finite $t\ge t_0$ and a request $r$ with
$n_t(r)=9$. For **every** $\lambda>0$ and
finite attained globally feasible dual $y$ satisfying (FC.5) for
which $r\in[r_-(t,\lambda),r_+(t,\lambda)]$, at least one of

$$
R_-\not\succeq0,\qquad a_o=b_o=0,\qquad
w_o^TR_+w_o<0
\tag{FC.51}
$$

must hold. In particular $R_-$ and $R_+$ cannot both be positive
semidefinite for any such dual. For a qualifying dual with
$R_-\succeq0$ and $(a_o,b_o)\ne(0,0)$, the last obstruction
means the zero line of $L_o$ passes through the open negative cone
of $R_+$, with slope in $(0,\chi)$.

Proof. If all three alternatives in (FC.51) fail for one qualifying
dual, (FC.48) holds and Theorem 14.1 gives $n_t(r)\le8$,
contrary to the premise. Both remainders PSD are excluded by
Theorem 14.2 even when $L_o=0$. In the last stated scope,
$w_o^TR_+w_o<0$ is a nonzero direction in the open negative
cone. Its slope is finite by (FC.45), so $b_o\ne0$; its slope
is $-a_o/b_o\in\mathcal I_-\subset(0,\chi)$ and
$L_o(w_o)=0$. This proves the zero-line assertion.
The contraposition applies separately to every stated multiplier
and dual. It neither assumes such a dual exists at every request
nor treats failure of a sufficient condition as proof of nine-low
necessity. $\square$

